with the tflite benchmark tool, i could profile a model which shows improvement by setting the 'num_threads' (from 0, 1, 2, 4), But trying to write the same in code, im not able to see the improvement by settings the num_threads. Anyone be able to help on this?

below is the code, Can anyone help on this?

``` 
 bool load_model(const std::string& model_path, bool use_xnnpack = true,
                    bool use_gpu = false, int num_threads = 1) {
        // Load the model using BuildFromFile
        model_ = tflite::FlatBufferModel::BuildFromFile(model_path.c_str());
        if (!model_) {
            return false;
        }

        // Create the interpreter
        tflite::ops::builtin::BuiltinOpResolver resolver;
        tflite::InterpreterBuilder builder(*model_, resolver);
        if (builder.SetNumThreads(num_threads) != kTfLiteOk) {
            TFLITE_LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to set thread number";
            return kTfLiteError;
        }
        builder(&interpreter_);
        if (!interpreter_) {
            return false;
        }
        // Apply XNNPACK delegate if requested
        if (use_xnnpack) {
            TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptions xnnpack_options =
                TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptionsDefault();
            xnnpack_delegate_ = TfLiteXNNPackDelegateCreate(&xnnpack_options);
            if (interpreter_->ModifyGraphWithDelegate(xnnpack_delegate_) !=
                kTfLiteOk) {
                std::cerr << "Failed to apply XNNPACK delegate." << std::endl;
                return false;
            }
        }
    }
```

If you are using the default delegate, then `tflite::InterpreterBuilder::SetNumThreads` is indeed the right way to set the number of threads.

However, if you are explicitly creating and applying an XNNPack delegate instance, then you need to be aware that `TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptions` also has an option for the number of threads in the XNNPack thread pool, and that defaults to zero, which means don't use a thread pool.  So by calling `TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptionsDefault()` and using the resulting options object to initialize the XNNPack delegate instance, you are implicitly turning off the use of threads.

When explicitly creating and applying an XNNPack delegate, you can set the `num_threads` member of the `TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptions` struct to set the number of threads.  E.g.
 
```
  TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptions xnnpack_options = TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptionsDefault();
  xnnpack_options.num_threads = 4;
```

Then you can construct and apply the delegate as usual:

```
  TfLiteDelegate* xnnpack_delegate = TfLiteXNNPackDelegateCreate(&xnnpack_options);
  tflite::ops::builtin::BuiltinOpResolver resolver;
  tflite::InterpreterBuilder builder(*model, resolver);
  builder.AddDelegate(xnnpack_delegate);
  TfLiteInterpreter interpreter;
  builder(&interpreter); 
  ...
  // Don't forget to also call TfLiteXXNpackDelegateDelete() when you're done with the interpreter.
```

